WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.337
This slide focuses on choosing only the columns you need.

00:00:03.337 --> 00:00:09.972
If a table contains many columns but the question only needs medical record number, patient name, and age,

00:00:09.972 --> 00:00:14.037
selecting those three columns makes the result easier to understand.

00:00:14.037 --> 00:00:16.487
Run the example and look at the output.

00:00:16.487 --> 00:00:21.547
Notice that the result is narrower than the full table.

00:00:21.547 --> 00:00:23.072
SELECT star is not wrong.

00:00:23.072 --> 00:00:26.702
It can be useful during exploration when you need to see the table structure.

00:00:26.702 --> 00:00:32.347
But in reports, applications, and reusable queries, explicit columns usually make your intent clearer.

00:00:32.347 --> 00:00:36.587
Also notice the sorting in this example.

00:00:36.587 --> 00:00:41.452
Before running it, predict who should appear first if the rows are sorted from the oldest patient to

00:00:41.452 --> 00:00:46.262
the youngest. Then compare your prediction with the result.

00:00:46.262 --> 00:00:51.272
Connect this idea to an application safely inserting or updating business records.

00:00:51.272 --> 00:00:56.932
Ask what could go wrong if the concept were misunderstood: could rows be duplicated,

00:00:56.932 --> 00:01:02.452
important records disappear, categories be interpreted incorrectly, or a report become difficult to reproduce?

00:01:02.452 --> 00:01:07.587
A strong analyst can explain both the SQL operation and the assumption behind it.

00:01:07.587 --> 00:01:13.269
After the example, summarize the idea in one plain-language sentence before moving on.
